{#
  ============================================================================
  CONSENT + ANALYTICS — the only integration point for these IDs
  ============================================================================

  *** THIS IS WHERE GTM / GA4 / USERCENTRICS ARE WIRED IN. ***

  No identifier is hardcoded here. All three come from environment variables,
  surfaced through src/_data/site.js. To activate analytics:

      1. Put the values in `.env` (local) or GitHub Actions variables (CI):

             USERCENTRICS_SETTINGS_ID=...      from the Usercentrics admin UI
             GTM_CONTAINER_ID=GTM-XXXXXXX
             GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX   (configured inside GTM;
                                                recorded for the privacy policy)

      2. Set SITE_ENV=production.

      3. Rebuild. This partial then emits the blocks below, in order.

  PHASE 9A (Usercentrics CMP + Autoblocker): ships with a separate
  Autoblocker script that must run before anything else in <head> (its job
  is intercepting third-party script insertion until a consent decision
  exists, which only works if it is the first thing to execute). The loader
  script's ID attribute is `data-settings-id` -- confirmed against the
  actual snippet in the Usercentrics admin panel (an earlier version of this
  file briefly used `data-ruleset-id` on the assumption that shipping with
  Autoblocker implied Usercentrics' newer ruleset-based product; that
  assumption was wrong, and is why the CMP failed to initialise -- Usercentrics
  themselves returned 403 "Forbidden" for a ruleset lookup against an ID that
  was actually a Settings ID). GTM itself is still the plain, unmodified
  standard snippet from Google -- installing it here does not configure GA4,
  Google Ads, or any other tag. Those are configured separately, inside GTM,
  by the project owner; this file has no way to create them and does not
  attempt to.

  Until BOTH the Usercentrics ID and the GTM ID exist, this partial renders
  NOTHING AT ALL. That is deliberate:

      - A half-configured state is worse than none. GTM without a CMP would load
        Google tags with no way to collect consent.
      - There is no stub, no fake banner and no placeholder consent system. The
        spec forbids implementing a fake consent mechanism, and a stub would give
        a false sense that consent had been handled.

  Analytics also stays off outside production even when IDs are present, so
  preview traffic never reaches the real GA4 property (see site.analytics.enabled).

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING — DO NOT REORDER (spec §8)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
      1. <meta charset>              in base.njk, above this include
      2. Usercentrics Autoblocker    THIS FILE, first -- must run before any
                                      third-party script tag exists to block
      3. Consent Mode v2 defaults    THIS FILE, second
      4. Usercentrics CMP loader     THIS FILE, third (data-settings-id)
      5. GTM container               THIS FILE, fourth -- unmodified standard
                                      snippet, loads no tags of its own

  GTM is itself a Google tag: it starts evaluating triggers the moment it loads.
  Consent Mode only works if the "nothing consented yet" state is already in the
  dataLayer before anything reads it. Usercentrics must initialise before GTM so
  a returning visitor's stored decision is applied rather than raced. Autoblocker
  must run before Usercentrics itself (and everything else) so it can intercept
  script tags from the moment the page starts parsing.

  On a comparable project this exact ordering bug shipped: the CMP was delivered
  as a tag *inside* GTM, so it could only run after GTM had already begun firing.
  Measured result: analytics scripts loading before any consent decision.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CONSENT MODE GATES GOOGLE TAGS ONLY
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Consent Mode v2 governs Google tags. It does NOT gate non-Google scripts —
  Meta Pixel, LinkedIn Insight, Microsoft Clarity and similar fire regardless of
  consent state unless each is given an explicit consent-based trigger in GTM or
  is blocked by Usercentrics directly. If any such tag is added later it must be
  gated explicitly and re-verified on a fresh visit.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  DEFAULTS ARE NOT INVENTED
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Everything denied except security_storage, plus ads_data_redaction. This is the
  most restrictive starting state; the CMP relaxes it only on an explicit
  decision. wait_for_update gives the CMP time to report a stored decision before
  Google tags act on the defaults.

  Google Ads in the EEA requires Consent Mode v2, so this is a prerequisite for
  the planned acquisition campaigns, not optional compliance decoration.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOT CMS-EDITABLE
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Consent configuration must never become editable content. Which tags require
  which consent category is managed in GTM and Usercentrics by the owner.

  LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: Usercentrics validates the requesting domain against an
  allow-list and throws CMP_NOT_ALLOWED on localhost. Consent behaviour cannot be
  verified locally — it needs an allow-listed preview domain (spec §15).
#}
{%- if site.analytics.enabled %}
    <!-- Usercentrics Autoblocker - must be the first script in <head> -->
    <script src="https://web.cmp.usercentrics.eu/modules/autoblocker.js"></script>
    <!-- End Usercentrics Autoblocker -->

    <!-- Consent Mode v2 defaults - must run before any Google tag -->
    <script>
    window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
    function gtag(){dataLayer.push(arguments);}
    gtag('consent', 'default', {
        'ad_storage': 'denied',
        'ad_user_data': 'denied',
        'ad_personalization': 'denied',
        'analytics_storage': 'denied',
        'functionality_storage': 'denied',
        'personalization_storage': 'denied',
        'security_storage': 'granted',
        'wait_for_update': 500
    });
    gtag('set', 'ads_data_redaction', true);
    </script>
    <!-- End Consent Mode v2 defaults -->

    <!-- Usercentrics CMP - must load before GTM -->
    <script id="usercentrics-cmp"
            src="https://web.cmp.usercentrics.eu/ui/loader.js"
            data-settings-id="{{ site.analytics.usercentricsSettingsId }}"
            async></script>
    <!-- End Usercentrics CMP -->

    <!-- Google Tag Manager - must come after the CMP -->
    <script>(function(w,d,s,l,i){w[l]=w[l]||[];w[l].push({'gtm.start':
    new Date().getTime(),event:'gtm.js'});var f=d.getElementsByTagName(s)[0],
    j=d.createElement(s),dl=l!='dataLayer'?'&l='+l:'';j.async=true;j.src=
    'https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtm.js?id='+i+dl;f.parentNode.insertBefore(j,f);
    })(window,document,'script','dataLayer','{{ site.analytics.gtmId }}');</script>
    <!-- End Google Tag Manager -->
{%- endif %}
